# Declarative build spec for the long-read-assembly `assemble` image (hifiasm_meta
# and myloasm, in separate envs — see assemble.def), consumed by
# scripts/build-sif.sh (the single generic SIF builder) via
# `build-sif.sh long-read-assembly assemble`. `source`d as bash — plain KEY="value"
# pairs, no logic. See CLAUDE.md "Container image tier".
#
# One of four per-STEP images the workflow ships (assemble / binning / bin_refine /
# checkm), each with its own def + spec so a change to one image's solve rebuilds
# ONLY that image.

# Output SIF filename, written to ${PATH_DERIVED}/images/. MUST match the
# `container:` value on the `assemble` step in 1.0.0.yaml (test_sif_build_spec.py
# enforces this).
SIF_FILENAME="long-read-assembly-assemble-1.0.0.sif"

# The def this image builds (workflow-relative). Distinguishes it from the other
# three per-step defs in the same workflow dir.
DEF_FILE="assemble.def"

# This image's in-repo build inputs (workflow-relative), used to SCOPE the
# idempotency hash: the def is auto-included and _shared/manifest_writer.py is
# always hashed, so this lists the entrypoint + shared helper it %files-copies.
# A change to a SIBLING tool's def/entrypoint then leaves this image unchanged.
# myloasm_split.py is %files-copied too and is what decides which contigs become
# LCGs — leaving it out would let an edited splitter be skipped as "unchanged".
# miint_connect.py is the module it imports for the staged-miint connection, so an
# edit there changes what the splitter does without touching the splitter.
HASH_INPUTS="assemble.sh myloasm_split.py miint_connect.py"

# No SOURCES: hifiasm_meta and myloasm are both fetched from bioconda in %post (no
# redistribution restriction), so the build stages nothing from the checkout.

# Idempotency + post-build verification. The builder runs
# `apptainer exec <sif> ${VERIFY_CMD}` (word-split, bare tokens — no eval, no
# `$`/backtick) and requires the output to match the extended-regex VERIFY_MATCH.
#
# This asserts myloasm's resolved VERSION rather than just that an env exists.
# The old `micromamba env list` / `assemble` pair proved only that an env of that
# name was created — it could not tell a correct image from one whose solve had
# drifted, and the version is precisely what this image's correctness hangs on:
# myloasm_split.py reads circularity out of a myloasm header string probed against
# 0.6.0. Running the binary also proves the env is usable, which listing it does
# not.
#
# hifiasm_meta is pinned too (assemble.def), and its two internal version strings
# are asserted in that def's %test rather than here. That is not the hole it looks
# like: the skip needs BOTH this match and an unchanged build-inputs digest, and
# the digest is seeded with the def itself (build-sif.sh `hash_files=("${DEF}")`),
# so bumping the hifiasm_meta pin forces a rebuild and runs those greps. What this
# spec alone would not catch is a SIF whose digest matches but whose contents
# drifted — which is the same residue myloasm's assert leaves.
#
# The `-n myloasm` env name MUST track the def's `micromamba create` lines. This
# spec is what the builder runs BOTH for the idempotency skip and the post-build
# re-verify, so a stale name here can never be skipped-as-built and then fails the
# re-verify — aborting the whole deploy before any service restarts, not just this
# image. A unit test asserts the name against the def for that reason.
#
# Unanchored literal, like the sibling `lima 2.13.0` / `bcl-convert Version 4.5.4`
# specs: test_sif_build_spec.py rejects a `$` anywhere in a spec value (it screens
# for shell substitution), so a `$`-anchored regex is not available here. The def's
# %test carries the exact-line form (`grep -Fxq`).
VERIFY_CMD="micromamba run -n myloasm myloasm --version"
VERIFY_MATCH="myloasm 0.6.0"
